think The live edge is a deep green cluster-tree family off the image scene-read floor; the card and the last logs name the crux-first agent-doable options, among them a leaf/internal read over the MST bridge edges. That is the plainest structural fact underneath every special-cluster read already built (poles, center, centroid), so it is the crux the family has circled.
think Studied the two closest templates (cluster_tree_centroid.rye, cluster_minimum_spanning_tree.rye) and the diameter/radius public APIs to compose, not braid: this rung owns only the degree read and the tip/joint partition, cross-checking the poles (diameter), center (radius), and centroid against their own modules never re-derived.
think Designed a SPROUT scene to prove the whole crux in one shape -- a main path 1-2-3-4 with a fifth cluster sprouting off interior joint 2. Worked the pixel geometry so Prim builds exactly edges 1-2, 2-3, 3-4, 2-5: consecutive main blocks 3 apart, the sprout 2 below cluster 2 and 3 from clusters 1 and 3, so it attaches to 2 alone.
obs The crux: the tip/joint partition is COARSER than pole/center/centroid and BOUNDS every one. On the sprout the poles are the path ends 1 and 4 (both leaves) while leaf 5's reach 8 falls short of the diameter 9 -- is_diameter_endpoint(5) FALSE though is_leaf(5) TRUE, so poles are a STRICT SUBSET of the leaves; on a path the two coincide. The center and centroid both land on the interior joint cluster 2, never a tip, because a leaf's removal is the worst balance and a leaf's reach the worst eccentricity.
obs GREEN on the FIRST full metal run -- the sprout geometry held on the first pass, no reds. The handshake lemma (degrees sum to twice the edge count) and the leaves+internal == clusters partition both assert positively inside partition_counts.
obs Version const read straight from the live clock 032203, no future stamp (REDS #75). TAME bans clean, width-check exit 0, plain 7-bit ASCII, living-card ASCII GREEN, witness native contains (REDS #85).
